                     /EC_number="6.4.1.2"
                     /function="Component of the acetyl coenzyme A carboxylase
                     (ACC) complex. Biotin carboxylase (BC) catalyzes the
                     carboxylation of biotin on its carrier protein (BCCP) and
                     then the CO(2) group is transferred by the
                     transcarboxylase to acetyl-CoA to form malonyl-CoA;
                     Controls translation of mRNA for both itself and the
                     alpha-subunit (accA) by binding to a probable hairpin in
                     the 5' of the mRNA. Binding to mRNA inhibits translation;
                     this is partially relieved by acetyl-CoA. Increasing
                     amounts of mRNA also inhibit enzyme activity"
